      "text": "- 7 Bruce Archer remarked that it was important to think of the work as being bottom up rather than top down. He pointed out that there is evidence that curriculum development is most successful where it is being generated in schools.\n- 8 Ken Baynes went on to explain that the form the project will take relates directly to the described background. It has not been possible to see an example of the design dimension in action yet. The most important immediate aim would be to identify perhaps twelve secondary schools with their feeder primaries where the teachers were willing to develop a design education policy accross the curriculum. He felt there was a need to limit the Project initially to six local authorities. The aim then would be to establish the idea of the design dimension which could continue after the Project had finished. The Project Team would work in each of the authorities to provide general help and guidance;a.rid to create a climate of participation. Findings would be published on work in progress.\n- 9 Bruce Archer asked if the criteria for selecting the schools was clear. Ken Baynes replied that the schools would have to be prepared to undertake planned change.\n- 10",
