      "archive_resolution_status": "archive_resolved_current",
      "title": "Bruce Archer paper 'An introduction to design research'",
      "page_start": 4,
      "page_end": 4,
      "chunk_id": "turin_doc_338541406157_1cb3d7b8e15f_4_25_ee52dce0c96f268f",
      "chunk_sequence": 25,
      "text": "These considerations apply to any kind of research. Most design research, however, indeed most research, is neither objective nor scientific, neither academic nor grant-aided. It is the sort of research which is sometimes called Action research. It begins with a problem which demands inform\u00ad ation or ideas. The search may on occasion be for information which is not new, by means which are not replicable and on grounds based in valuejudgement. Insofar as it allows decisions to be made or action to be taken, it suffices. This is mainly research for the purposes of design, and it mostly falls into the class of design information. It is mainly undertaken by designers, or by specialists for the benefit of designers, in the course of ordinary design projects. Research into design embraces the study of design activity, design sensibility and the design scene. It is undertaken by design researchers for the ultimate benefit of design practitioners, managers, teachers and so on. In order to earn its support, usually grantaided, it must generally take an academic or scholarly form. Whether academic or action in type, however, all design research has features which distinguish it from most",
