      "chunk_sequence": 723,
      "text": "With the recent developments in computer aided building design it has frequently been pointed out that computer aids perform design activities which cut across the traditionally accepted bound\u00ad aries of professional competence, the client organisation and institutions such as statutory authorities. Although the assessment of the impact of computer aids on professional practice might only take place by a slow trial and error process, the SHADO model should be a useful tool to anticipate the impact of proposed comp\u00ad uter aids at an early stage in their proposed application as disc\u00ad ussed in Chapter 5.1. Moreover it would help towards cost\u00ad benefit studies undertaken to assess the economic feasibility of computer aids. It is increasingly argued that such economic factors cannot be adequately assessed within the narrow confines of time saving, drudgery reduction and design optimisation. A recent feasibility study for the application of computer aided design in the Consortium for Method Building (Garnett 1970) pointed out: \"Some of the constituent activities in each profession will be more affected by the system than will others, e. g. draughting. However, we do not have sufficient",
